---

<BaseLayout
  title="AI Summer Camp, 2026 — a retrospective"
  description="Students could publish polished work in minutes. The teaching problem started there."
>
  <article class="retro">
    <p class="draft-stamp">draft · under review</p>

    <header data-mark="retro:masthead">
      <h1>The work looked finished before the thinking was.</h1>
      <p class="dek">
        A summer camp on working with AI, for middle and high school students.
        What happened when making things became almost free.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section data-mark="retro:it-worked">
      <h2>It worked</h2>
      <p>
        In the first days of camp, students published real software. They kept daily
        journals in Google Docs, fed them into NotebookLM — an AI notebook that answers
        from sources you give it — then attached those notebooks to a chat, generated
        small web applications with Gemini Canvas, and deployed them to the public web.
        Many built games. Most of the results were impressive.
      </p>
      <blockquote>
        <p>
          "They had already been keeping a daily journal, which I had them feed into
          NotebookLM to see how they could update it and then sync it to the notebook when
          it changed. I also had them attach a notebook to a chat, then add Canvas to
          create a thing, which I actually had them deploy to Netlify. A lot were games;
          most were impressive. But did it demonstrate a tremendous amount of agency on
          behalf of the kids? They did produce something, though."
        </p>
        <cite>Teaching note, July 8. Typos cleaned; wording unchanged.</cite>
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        The artifacts created real energy. They exceeded what anyone expects students to
        make in an afternoon. That success is where the teaching problem started.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section data-mark="retro:who-decided">
      <h2>Then the question: who decided?</h2>
      <p>
        A published project could be inspected. The student's decisions inside it often
        could not. Which choices could the student locate, explain, and change? The work
        could look complete before the student had made many consequential decisions at
        all. The problem emerged from AI's success, not from its failure.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section data-mark="retro:response">
      <h2>The instructional response: productive friction</h2>
      <p>
        The response was not to prohibit AI or pretend its leverage away. The course
        progressively required students to supply what the model could not legitimately
        supply for them: intention, lived material, criteria, evidence, taste, rejection,
        judgment. Each technique returned a different responsibility to the student.
      </p>
      <ul class="techniques">
        <li><strong>Handwriting first.</strong> Language, images, and uncertainty that existed before any generation.</li>
        <li><strong>A daily journal.</strong> Continuity outside the chat — a source that changes over time.</li>
        <li><strong>An intent interview.</strong> Audience, purpose, desired effect, constraints — a brief the student can correct.</li>
        <li><strong>Define "better" first.</strong> Criteria and a predicted failure, before the model supplies a standard implicitly.</li>
        <li><strong>AI as reader, not writer.</strong> The model interprets material the student already made; the student accepts, corrects, or rejects the reading.</li>
        <li><strong>Source grounding.</strong> Evidence capable of contradicting fluent model language.</li>
        <li><strong>Peer response.</strong> An audience outside the closed student-and-AI loop.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep, reject, or redirect.</strong> Taste expressed as an observable decision, with a reason.</li>
        <li><strong>Revision.</strong> Proof that reflection had a consequence in the artifact.</li>
        <li><strong>Presentation and defense.</strong> Public ownership without pretending the student made every component.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        These responsibilities accumulate: a student grounding claims in sources is still
        expected to be originating material, articulating intent, and making choices.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section data-mark="retro:failed-friction">
      <h2>Not all friction worked</h2>
      <blockquote>
        <p>
          "I'm doing it now and it is going terribly. No one is engaged. Everyone is
          confused, and the prompt is taking too long to answer. It is chaos and boredom
          at once."
        </p>
        <cite>Teaching note, July 3, mid-class. Typos cleaned; wording unchanged.</cite>
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        A long AI-guided exercise meant to reconstruct authorship consumed attention
        without returning any decision to the students — friction that was merely
        procedural. The design changed the next day: shorter exchanges, human
        conversation where it worked better, one question at a time, and capture only of
        decisions that mattered.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section data-mark="retro:assessment">
      <h2>The assessment changed</h2>
      <p>By the final week, the capstone questions were different in kind:</p>
      <blockquote>
        <p>"Where did you spend the most effort?"</p>
        <p>"What was the most important decision you made?"</p>
        <cite>Capstone planning, July 9.</cite>
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        Attention moved from whether the artifact worked to where the student's effort
        and judgment entered it. That shift in instructional attention is what the
        summer's record currently supports. Stronger claims — that every student's later
        work became more distinctive — would need student artifacts and decision traces,
        collected with consent. Student work will appear here as each family approves it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section data-mark="retro:invitation">
      <h2>The open question</h2>
      <p class="invitation">
        When production becomes almost frictionless, which carefully chosen forms of
        friction help students become more visible inside their own work?
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
  <MarkNote />
</BaseLayout>

